/* libjbn profile: x86-64, no extension (runs on every x86-64 CPU, and
   under Rosetta). Required once, at the top level of a program, before the
   instances (README, "Profiles and the selection of the multiplication").

   JBN_FEATURES:   no BMI2/ADX, so the multiplication rows use MUL/ADC.
   JBN_REG_MAX:    0 (bn_mulxr, the module it selects, needs BMI2/ADX).
   JBN_UNROLL_MAX: the multiplication, squaring and Montgomery reduction of
                   a size scope (bn_mulsel@n.jinc) are unrolled when
                   nlimbs <= JBN_UNROLL_MAX, and loop over their rows above.
                   64: on an Intel Core i7-14700K the unrolled bn_mul is
                   within 3% of the looped bn_mul_loop on an exponentiation
                   up to 16 limbs, and faster above (8% at 32 limbs, 16% at
                   64); sizes above 64 limbs were not timed (unrolled, a
                   96-limb field takes about 5 minutes to compile).
   JBN_SQR_UNROLL_MAX: the squaring of a size scope is the unrolled one
                   (the product-scanning squaring of bn_mul) up to this
                   size, also where the multiplication and the reduction
                   loop. 64, as JBN_UNROLL_MAX: the two were not timed
                   apart without MULX. Above it, a field bound with
                   fp_mul_ksqr@fp.jinc takes the squaring over the half
                   size (bn_ksqr@2n.jinc), in a size scope that has a half
                   scope (not timed in a field without MULX).
   JBN_EXP_WINDOW: the constant-time exponentiation of a field (_expm_ of
                   bn_exp@exp.jinc): 0 for the Montgomery ladder, which
                   needs no table; otherwise the fixed window of 4 bits,
                   with a table of 16*nlimbs words on the stack.
                   4: on an Intel Core i7-14700K the window takes 0.61 to
                   0.72 of the time of the ladder over bn_mul and
                   bn_mul_loop, from 4 to 64 limbs.
   A size scope may shadow JBN_UNROLL_MAX, JBN_SQR_UNROLL_MAX and
   JBN_EXP_WINDOW (`param int JBN_UNROLL_MAX = 0;` and
   `param int JBN_SQR_UNROLL_MAX = 0;` inside it, for instance, for the
   smallest code at that size; `param int JBN_EXP_WINDOW = 0;` for the
   smallest stack).
*/

from Libjbn require "jbn_features.jinc"

param int JBN_FEATURES = 0;
param int JBN_REG_MAX = 0;
param int JBN_UNROLL_MAX = 64;
param int JBN_SQR_UNROLL_MAX = 64;
param int JBN_EXP_WINDOW = 4;
